CORE
JmlKotlinDataClass
--function "experiment.JmlKotlinDataClass.isViewable:(Lexperiment/ColumnAttributes;)Z" --jml-source src/main/kotlin/JmlKotlinDataClass.kt --unwind 5 -cp target/classes
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR
^.*FAILURE$
--
Regression for JML field access on Kotlin data classes.

Kotlin's `data class ColumnAttributes(val viewable: Boolean, ...)`
emits private final boolean fields plus public getX() accessors.
JML on `attrs.viewable` resolves to a member_exprt against the
private field — Java-level access modifiers don't gate JBMC's
bytecode-level analysis, but the field's type (c_bool, width 8)
does require coercion to bool when used directly as a JML
predicate (otherwise downstream's equal_exprt construction
trips on type mismatch).

Exercises three coordinated improvements at once:
  1. obj.field resolution via jml_field_access (this commit's
     siblings: jml-this-field, jml-inherited-field).
  2. c_bool → bool coercion at the requires/ensures boundary
     (so `requires attrs.viewable` works as a logical predicate
     even though Java's boolean is c_bool_typet width 8).
  3. Null-literal pointer typing for `attrs != null` against
     a struct-tagged pointer parameter (parser produces null
     with empty pointee type; resolver retypes to match the
     comparison peer).
